WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.667
Question 0009: 16 people, 16 boxes
and one helper. Junior level.

2
00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:08.400
Two parts: the strategy,
then the code that carries it out.

3
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:14.433
A room with 16 boxes, each holding a note
with a number from 1 to 16, each number once.

4
00:00:14.433 --> 00:00:19.600
The small number is the box, the big one
is the note inside: box 1 holds note 6.

5
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:25.133
Person 0 goes in first: he looks into every box
and may swap notes up to 4 times.

6
00:00:25.133 --> 00:00:30.533
Then persons 1 to 16, one at a time:
each opens up to 8 boxes, moving nothing.

7
00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:35.833
The goal: everybody must find their own note.
If one fails, everybody loses.

8
00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:41.733
Part A: which strategy guarantees
that everybody wins, for any arrangement?

9
00:00:43.233 --> 00:00:47.333
The idea: every person first opens
the box with his own number.

10
00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:51.733
Then the box whose number is written
on the note he found, and so on.

11
00:00:51.733 --> 00:00:57.000
Let's follow person 1: box 1 holds note 6,
then 11, 3, 14, 9, 16 and 4.

12
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.167
8 opens are used up and note 1 is still missing.
It is in box 8, the 16th in the chain.

13
00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:07.967
Following the notes from any box,
the arrows always close into cycles.

14
00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:13.467
Here all 16 boxes are on one cycle:
box 1 points to 6, 6 to 11, and so on.

15
00:01:13.467 --> 00:01:18.967
Why cycles? One arrow leaves each box
and one enters it: every note appears once.

16
00:01:18.967 --> 00:01:23.067
Person i starts at box i,
so he walks along that box's cycle.

17
00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:27.733
Note i is in the box that points back
to box i: the last one on the cycle.

18
00:01:27.733 --> 00:01:32.467
So whoever is on a cycle of length L finds
his note at exactly the L-th open.

19
00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:37.500
A cycle of up to 8: everybody on it succeeds.
Here L = 16: all 16 people fail.

20
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:43.033
Without person 0 this happens a lot: the strategy
succeeds in only about a third of arrangements.

21
00:01:43.033 --> 00:01:47.433
And opening 8 random boxes is far worse:
it almost never works.

22
00:01:47.933 --> 00:01:52.833
Now person 0 comes in. He sees every box,
so he sees the cycle.

23
00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:58.300
He swaps the note of box 1, the start of the cycle,
with the note of box 12, in the middle.

24
00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:01.967
Note 6 moves to box 12,
and note 7 moves to box 1.

25
00:02:01.967 --> 00:02:07.367
Only two arrows change, and the cycle splits
into two cycles, each of length 8.

26
00:02:07.367 --> 00:02:12.967
Person 1 now: 1, 7, 2, 15, 5, 10, 13 and 8.
His note is at the eighth open.

27
00:02:12.967 --> 00:02:17.700
So everybody finds his note at exactly
the 8th open: everybody wins.

28
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:23.733
Why does it work for any arrangement? First fact:
there is at most one long cycle.

29
00:02:23.733 --> 00:02:29.133
The cycle lengths add up to 16, and two cycles
over 8 would already need 18 boxes.

30
00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:34.633
Second fact: swapping the notes of c0
and ch cuts the cycle into two cycles.

31
00:02:34.633 --> 00:02:38.133
One of length L − h, the other of length h.

32
00:02:38.733 --> 00:02:44.200
Choose the middle, h = ⌊L/2⌋. Since L ≤ 16,
both pieces have length 8 at most.

33
00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:48.867
So one swap is always enough,
and the other three swaps are not needed.

34
00:02:49.667 --> 00:02:53.900
A second arrangement, the page's example:
the notes in boxes 1 to 16.

35
00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:59.100
Following the arrows gives three cycles:
of lengths 11, 3 and 2.

36
00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:04.700
Person 13 is on the long cycle: 13, 1, 7, 12,
3, 15, 9 and 4. 8 opens are used up.

37
00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:08.200
Note 13 is in box 5,
the 11th on the cycle: too far.

38
00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.767
Person 0 swaps box 1 and box 9:
they are c0 and c5, since ⌊11/2⌋ = 5.

39
00:03:13.767 --> 00:03:19.767
Note 7 moves to box 9, and note 4 to box 1.
The cycle of 11 splits into 6 and 5.

40
00:03:19.767 --> 00:03:25.500
And now person 13: 13, 1, 4, 16, 10 and 5.
Box 5 holds his note, at the sixth open.

41
00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:29.900
And so for all 16 people: each of them
needs 6 opens at most.

42
00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:35.667
The page's box has the same arrangement. The dashed
arrows are person 0's swap.

43
00:03:35.667 --> 00:03:40.267
With each click person 13 opens a box:
first box 13, which holds note 1.

44
00:03:40.267 --> 00:03:43.733
He follows the notes:
box 1, then box 4.

45
00:03:43.733 --> 00:03:47.700
And at the sixth open, in box 5,
he finds his note.

46
00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:52.667
Without person 0 the cycle of 11 is red again,
and person 13 fails after 8 opens.

47
00:03:53.466 --> 00:03:58.666
Part B: implement the room with its limits,
person 0, everybody else, and a whole game.

48
00:03:59.366 --> 00:04:04.266
First the room, briefly and not typed:
the class Room that enforces the rules.

49
00:04:04.266 --> 00:04:08.666
The limits: 8 opens and 4 swaps.
The room keeps a private copy of the notes.

50
00:04:08.666 --> 00:04:13.466
look_all and swap only for person 0, before
anybody else enters, and up to 4 swaps.

51
00:04:13.466 --> 00:04:18.566
enter gives everyone who enters 8 opens, and open_box
counts them: the room enforces the rules.

52
00:04:19.666 --> 00:04:24.566
Now the strategy. First a helper function,
shared by person 0 and everybody else.

53
00:04:26.533 --> 00:04:30.066
cycle_from takes the notes
and a starting box.

54
00:04:32.933 --> 00:04:37.433
Start a list with the starting box,
and move to the box its note names.

55
00:04:41.766 --> 00:04:46.266
Until we are back at the start: add the box,
and follow its note.

56
00:04:47.466 --> 00:04:49.966
At the end, return the whole cycle.

57
00:04:49.966 --> 00:04:53.933
In the page's example, from box 2:
2, 11, 6, and back to 2.

58
00:04:57.599 --> 00:05:01.199
Person 0 is the function helper,
which takes the room.

59
00:05:04.133 --> 00:05:08.633
He reads all the notes at once,
and remembers which boxes he has visited.

60
00:05:16.633 --> 00:05:21.133
He goes over the boxes, skips those already on a cycle,
and finds the cycle of each new box.

61
00:05:26.866 --> 00:05:31.366
A cycle longer than 8: swap the first and the middle,
and leave. There is no second long cycle.

62
00:05:32.066 --> 00:05:34.866
Let's run person 0 on the page's example.

63
00:05:34.866 --> 00:05:39.666
The cycle of box 1 is the long cycle,
with 11 boxes.

64
00:05:39.666 --> 00:05:45.333
11 is longer than 8: he swaps cycle[0] = 1
and cycle[5] = 9, and leaves. One swap.

65
00:05:49.066 --> 00:05:53.566
Everybody else is find_my_note:
it takes the room and his own number.

66
00:05:55.466 --> 00:05:59.966
Enter, and start at your own box: that way
you are on the cycle of your note.

67
00:06:05.833 --> 00:06:09.633
Up to 8 times, open a box,
and if the note is yours, you found it.

68
00:06:11.766 --> 00:06:16.266
Otherwise move to the box on the note. After
8 opens without the note: failed.

69
00:06:16.966 --> 00:06:19.633
Person 13, after person 0's swap.

70
00:06:19.633 --> 00:06:24.599
He starts at box 13, which holds note 1:
not his, so he moves to box 1.

71
00:06:24.599 --> 00:06:29.266
Then boxes 1, 4, 16 and 10,
with notes 4, 16, 10 and 5.

72
00:06:29.266 --> 00:06:33.933
Box 5 holds note 13: he found it,
at the sixth open.

73
00:06:34.733 --> 00:06:39.766
play builds a room, runs person 0, then
everybody else, and checks that all 16 found theirs.

74
00:06:39.766 --> 00:06:44.433
The code was tested on all 231 cycle structures
and on 20,000 random arrangements.

75
00:06:44.433 --> 00:06:48.533
In all of them all 16 people won,
with at most one swap.

76
00:06:49.333 --> 00:06:54.733
The cost: person 0 takes O(N) time and memory,
and each of the others O(1) memory.

77
00:06:55.526 --> 00:07:00.626
To sum up: the notes form cycles, and person 0
cuts the long cycle with one swap.

78
00:07:00.626 --> 00:07:05.526
Try your own arrangements in the box
on the page of question 0009 on the site.
